From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Max Thomson <git@mnt.dev>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 19:42:21 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [routing] Online car router backed by Google

Adds RouterType::Google, an IRouter that reads no offline map data: it POSTs
the checkpoints to routes.googleapis.com/directions/v2:computeRoutes and
assembles a Route from the encoded polyline and per-step maneuvers. Traffic
comes back as speedReadingIntervals and is mapped onto traffic::SpeedGroup, so
congestion is drawn on the route line. Alternative routes are kept in the
order the API returns them.

The route is then re-derived from the OSM road graph rather than drawn as a
raw overlay, so it keeps native street names, lane guidance, turn annotations
and speed-camera warnings. Matching runs one A* per leg constrained to a
corridor around Google's polyline -- see corridor_matcher.hpp for why an
earlier design that resampled the reference into anchors and ran an
independent A* per anchor pair was abandoned -- with lateral-deviation and
length-ratio acceptance gates. A leg that fails either gate falls back to the
raw geometry overlay, as does a route for which no matcher is configured or
whose area has no maps downloaded.

The API key is entered by the user in Android settings or the Qt routing
dialog and stored under "GoogleApiKey" in the native settings store. Without a
key nothing is requested, so the feature is inert by default.

Also restores Route's move operations, which a user-declared copy constructor
had silently suppressed, turning every std::move(route) into a deep copy of
the geometry and segments.
